A shared channel with multiple senders has a coordination problem: close means 'no one will ever send again', but each sender knows only its own intent. The two canonical solutions:

Fan-in pattern — a dedicated merger owns the output:

```go
func merge(outputs ...<-chan int) <-chan int {
    out := make(chan int)
    var wg sync.WaitGroup
    wg.Add(len(outputs))
    for _, ch := range outputs {
        go func(c <-chan int) {
            defer wg.Done()
            for v := range c {   // each input closes normally
                out <- v
            }
        }(ch)
    }
    go func() {
        wg.Wait()      // ALL forwarding goroutines done
        close(out)    // now, and only now: nobody will send
    }()
    return out
}
```

Each upstream producer closes its own channel (its prerogative — it's the sole sender there). The merge goroutines drain those inputs; the closer waits for every one to finish, then closes the single output once. Exactly one close of the shared channel, executed by the one goroutine that has observed every sender finish.

WaitGroup-at-source pattern — when the senders write directly into a shared channel:

```go
var wg sync.WaitGroup
for i := 0; i < 3; i++ {
    wg.Add(1)
    go func(id int) {
        defer wg.Done()
        for _, v := range work(id) {
            results <- v
        }
    }(i)
}
go func() {
    wg.Wait()
    close(results)
}()
```

Same law, different placement: the closer waits on the count of all senders. What both patterns share — and what the interview answer should name — is that 'who closes' is answered by 'who can PROVE production ended', and with multiple producers, proof is a synchronization primitive (WaitGroup) or an architecture (fan-in), never a per-sender close.
